---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
---

<DocShell
   title="Criterion lookup"
   summary="Lookup is the front door. If an agent skips it and starts poking at the page blindly, the rest of the run gets sloppy fast."
   links={docsLinks}
>
   <section>
      <h2>The actual commands</h2>
      <pre><code>node packages/cli/dist/cli.js wcag levels --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js wcag criteria --level AA --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js wcag show 4.1.3 --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js wcag search "status message" --json
node packages/cli/dist/cli.js wcag coverage 4.1.3 --json</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>What lookup returns</h2>
      <ul>
         <li>the criterion id, title, and level</li>
         <li>coverage state and linked ACT or axe coverage where it exists</li>
         <li>the verification strategy the runtime will follow</li>
         <li>enough structured text to explain the criterion without re-scraping W3C pages</li>
      </ul>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>When to use each one</h2>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag levels</code><span>Ask what conformance targets exist for the selected WCAG version.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag criteria --level AA</code><span>Get the rows that belong to a level before you ask for a full level verification.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag show 4.1.3</code><span>Resolve one criterion by id or slug.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag search "status message"</code><span>Recover the right criterion when you know the concept but not the id.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>wcag coverage 4.1.3</code><span>See whether the runtime treats the criterion as automated, hybrid, or manual-only.</span></div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <h2>Good habit</h2>
      <p>
         Resolve by id when you can. Search is for recovery, not for avoiding specificity. "Check AA"
         is a reasonable starting point. "Poke around and see what seems accessible" is not.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
